WEBVTT

00:00.249 --> 00:01.851
- As a little bit of
a review, we know that

00:01.851 --> 00:04.754
if we have some function,
let's call it "f".

00:04.754 --> 00:06.461
We don't have to call it
"f", but "f" is the letter

00:06.461 --> 00:08.655
most typically used for functions,

00:08.655 --> 00:11.221
that if I give it an input, a valid input,

00:11.221 --> 00:14.785
if I give it a valid input,
and I use the variable "x"

00:14.785 --> 00:19.785
for that valid input, it is
going to map that to an output.

00:19.995 --> 00:21.992
It is going to map that, or produce,

00:21.992 --> 00:24.453
given this x, it's going
to product an output

00:24.453 --> 00:26.926
that we would call "f(x)."

00:26.926 --> 00:28.087
And we've already talked a little bit

00:28.087 --> 00:29.921
about the notion of a domain.

00:29.921 --> 00:32.882
A domain is the set of all of the inputs

00:32.882 --> 00:35.088
over which the function is defined.

00:35.088 --> 00:39.291
So if this the domain here,
if this is the domain here,

00:39.291 --> 00:42.657
and I take a value here,
and I put that in for x,

00:42.657 --> 00:45.386
then the function is
going to output an f(x).

00:45.386 --> 00:47.917
If I take something that's
outside of the domain,

00:47.917 --> 00:49.693
let me do that in a different color...

00:49.693 --> 00:52.723
If I take something that
is outside of the domain

00:52.723 --> 00:55.161
and try to input it into this function,

00:55.161 --> 00:56.322
the function will say, "hey, wait wait,"

00:56.322 --> 00:57.994
"I'm not defined for that thing"

00:57.994 --> 01:00.061
"that's outside of the domain."

01:00.061 --> 01:04.081
Now another interesting
thing to think about,

01:04.081 --> 01:06.258
and that's actually what
the focus of this video is,

01:06.258 --> 01:08.661
okay, we know the set of
all of the valid inputs,

01:08.661 --> 01:10.960
that's called the domain,
but what about all,

01:10.960 --> 01:13.293
the set of all of the
outputs that the function

01:13.293 --> 01:14.861
could actually produce?

01:14.861 --> 01:16.591
And we have a name for that.

01:16.591 --> 01:19.551
That is called the range of the function.

01:19.551 --> 01:20.771
So the range.

01:21.659 --> 01:23.690
The range, and the most typical,

01:23.690 --> 01:25.386
there's actually a couple
of definitions for range,

01:25.386 --> 01:27.359
but the most typical definition for range

01:27.359 --> 01:30.018
is "the set of all possible outputs."

01:30.018 --> 01:32.514
So you give me, you input
something from the domain,

01:32.514 --> 01:34.894
it's going to output
something, and by definition,

01:34.894 --> 01:36.856
because we have outputted
it from this function,

01:36.856 --> 01:39.016
that thing is going to be in the range,

01:39.016 --> 01:40.722
and if we take the set
of all of the things

01:40.722 --> 01:42.115
that the function could output,

01:42.115 --> 01:44.821
that is going to make up the range.

01:44.821 --> 01:49.755
So this right over here is
the set of all possible,

01:49.755 --> 01:53.284
all possible outputs.

01:53.284 --> 01:55.188
All possible outputs.

01:55.188 --> 01:57.023
So let's make that a
little bit more concrete,

01:57.023 --> 01:58.590
with an example.

01:58.590 --> 02:02.317
So let's say that I have the function f(x)

02:02.317 --> 02:06.195
defined as, so once again,
I'm gonna input x's,

02:06.195 --> 02:10.990
and I have my function f,
and I'm gonna output f(x).

02:10.990 --> 02:12.359
And let's say this def...

02:12.359 --> 02:13.950
The function definition
here, the thing that tries to

02:13.950 --> 02:16.620
figure out, "okay, given an
x, what f(x) do I produce?",

02:16.620 --> 02:20.359
the definition says "f(x)
is going to be equal"

02:20.359 --> 02:23.284
"to whatever my input is, squared."

02:23.284 --> 02:24.562
Well, just as a little bit of review,

02:24.562 --> 02:26.988
we know what the domain
here is going to be.

02:26.988 --> 02:30.959
The domain is the set of all valid inputs.

02:30.959 --> 02:33.118
So what are the valid inputs here?

02:33.118 --> 02:34.894
Well, I could take any real number

02:34.894 --> 02:37.251
and input into this, and I
could take any real number

02:37.251 --> 02:39.388
and I can square it, there's
nothing wrong with that,

02:39.388 --> 02:42.151
and so the domain is all real numbers.

02:42.151 --> 02:47.151
All, all real, all real numbers.

02:48.153 --> 02:49.825
But what's the range?

02:49.825 --> 02:50.855
Maybe I'll do that in a different color

02:50.855 --> 02:52.260
just to highlight it.

02:52.260 --> 02:54.025
What is going to be the range here,

02:54.025 --> 02:57.209
what is the set of all possible outputs?

02:58.190 --> 02:59.490
Well if you think about, actually,

02:59.490 --> 03:02.160
to help us think about, let
me actually draw a graph here.

03:02.160 --> 03:04.157
Of what this looks like.

03:04.157 --> 03:06.224
What this looks like.

03:06.224 --> 03:08.546
So the graph of "f(x)
is equal to x squared"

03:08.546 --> 03:10.287
is going to look something like this.

03:10.287 --> 03:15.024
So, it's gonna look, it's going
to look something like this.

03:15.024 --> 03:17.056
I'm obviously hand-drawing
it, so it's not perfect.

03:17.056 --> 03:19.320
It's gonna be a parabola with a,

03:19.320 --> 03:22.153
with a vertex right here at the origin.

03:22.153 --> 03:24.591
So this is the graph, this is the graph,

03:24.591 --> 03:28.921
"y is equal to f(x)," this
of course is the x-axis,

03:28.921 --> 03:31.592
this of course is the y-axis.

03:31.592 --> 03:33.089
So let's think about it, what is the set

03:33.089 --> 03:35.052
of all possible outputs?

03:35.052 --> 03:36.723
Well in this case, the set
of all possible outputs

03:36.723 --> 03:38.952
is the set of all possible y's here.

03:38.952 --> 03:43.295
Well, we see, y can take
on any non-negative value.

03:43.295 --> 03:47.857
y could be zero, y could one,
y could be pi, y could be e,

03:47.857 --> 03:51.027
but y cannot be negative.

03:51.027 --> 03:54.324
So the range here is, the range...

03:54.324 --> 03:56.124
We could, well we could
say it a couple of ways,

03:56.124 --> 03:59.189
we could say, "f(x)",
let me write it this way.

03:59.189 --> 04:03.252
"f(x) is a member of the real numbers"

04:03.252 --> 04:08.252
"such that, is such that
f(x) is greater than"

04:09.823 --> 04:11.391
"or equal to zero."

04:11.391 --> 04:13.051
We could write it that
way, if we wanted to

04:13.051 --> 04:15.559
write it in a less mathy notation,

04:15.559 --> 04:19.355
we could say that "f(x) is going to be"

04:19.355 --> 04:21.921
"greater than or equal to zero."

04:21.921 --> 04:24.185
f(x) is not going to be negative,

04:24.185 --> 04:27.621
so any non-negative number, the set of all

04:27.621 --> 04:31.824
non-negative numbers, that is our range.

04:31.824 --> 04:33.055
Let's do another example of this,

04:33.055 --> 04:35.981
just to make it a little bit,
just to make it a little bit,

04:35.981 --> 04:37.420
a little bit clearer.

04:37.420 --> 04:42.250
Let's say that I had,
let's say that I had g(x),

04:42.250 --> 04:45.153
let's say I have g(x),
I'll do this in white,

04:45.153 --> 04:49.820
let's say it's equal
to "x squared over x."

04:49.820 --> 04:52.595
So we could try to
simplify g(x) a little bit,

04:52.595 --> 04:53.825
we could say, "look, if I have x squared"

04:53.825 --> 04:55.590
"and I divide it by x, that's gonna,"

04:55.590 --> 04:59.653
"that's the same thing as
g(x) being equal to x."

04:59.653 --> 05:02.347
"x squared over x" is x,
but we have to be careful.

05:02.347 --> 05:04.657
Because right over here, we have to,

05:04.657 --> 05:08.094
in our domain, x cannot be equal to zero.

05:08.094 --> 05:10.393
If x is equal to zero,
we get zero over zero,

05:10.393 --> 05:11.786
we get indeterminate form.

05:11.786 --> 05:14.828
So in order for this function
to be the exact same function,

05:14.828 --> 05:16.987
we have to put that,
'cause it's not obvious now

05:16.987 --> 05:21.204
from the definition, we have to say,

05:21.204 --> 05:23.988
"x cannot be equal to zero."

05:23.988 --> 05:25.985
So g(x) is equal to x for any x

05:25.985 --> 05:28.284
as long as x is not equal to zero.

05:28.284 --> 05:31.291
Now these two function
definitions are equivalent.

05:31.291 --> 05:32.710
And we could even graph it.

05:33.551 --> 05:35.455
We could graph it, it's going to look,

05:35.455 --> 05:38.961
I'm gonna do a quick and
dirty version of this graph.

05:38.961 --> 05:40.784
It's gonna look something like, this.

05:40.784 --> 05:43.222
It's gonna have a slope of one,

05:43.222 --> 05:44.859
but it's gonna have a hole right at zero,

05:44.859 --> 05:47.019
'cause it's not defined at zero.

05:47.019 --> 05:48.958
So it's gonna look like this.

05:48.958 --> 05:53.625
So the domain here, the domain of g

05:53.625 --> 05:57.027
is going to be, "x is a
member of the real numbers"

05:57.027 --> 06:00.486
"such that x does not equal zero,"

06:00.486 --> 06:03.853
and the range is actually
going to be the same thing.

06:03.853 --> 06:06.407
The range here is going to be,

06:06.407 --> 06:10.761
we could say "f(x) is a
member of the real numbers"

06:10.761 --> 06:14.860
"such that f(x) does not equal zero."

06:14.860 --> 06:17.692
"f(x) does not equal zero."

06:17.692 --> 06:19.852
So the domain is all real
numbers except for zero,

06:19.852 --> 06:24.852
the range is all real
numbers except for zero.

06:24.983 --> 06:28.118
So the big takeaway here is
the range is all the pos...

06:28.118 --> 06:32.054
The set of all possible
outputs of your function.

06:32.054 --> 06:35.061
The domain is the set of all valid inputs

06:35.061 --> 06:36.472
into your function.
